{
  "dom_id": "step-q105666",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)=2-{x}^{2}.$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that $f(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $x\\in [-3,1]?$",
  "worked_solution": "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-3,1],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-3)$ and $f(1).$\n\nSince $f(-3)=-7$ and $f(1)=1,$ we must have \n$k\\in [-7,1].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=-2$ belongs to the interval $[-7,1].$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/assets/3a9cf79e319e3a4a8d7d5195d65bc01e69bceb3721c29ca905132c1a607ba413.png)",
